"""Independently authored count and waiting-time problems; all TeX is raw."""
from pathlib import Path
import json
B=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
Q=[]
def q(title,stem,solution,model=None,origin=None):
 d=dict(id='s-distributions-original-'+str(len(Q)+1),title=title,stem=stem,solution=solution,origin=origin or 'Original formula and conceptual examination-style problem',difficulty='Medium to hard',visualDecision='Algebra and the explicitly stated experiment are sufficient; no decorative animation.')
 if model:d.update(modelId=model,visualDecision='A mechanism-specific companion shows the model assumptions and exact state transitions.',visualQualification='The companion title gives its sample parameters. It illustrates the stated mechanism; the written solution establishes the result for this question.')
 Q.append(d)
q('Recover a binomial experiment',r'A binomial count has mean $6$ and variance $4$. Recover its parameters and explain why both moments are needed.',r'For a nondegenerate binomial variable, $\mu=np$ and $v=np(1-p)$. Dividing gives $1-p=v/\mu=2/3$, hence $p=1/3$ and $n=18$. Both recovered parameters satisfy their domains. A mean of six alone admits infinitely many trial counts and probabilities, including $n=6,p=1$. The variance resolves that ambiguity within the binomial family; it does not prove that the unspecified experiment is binomial. Mutual independence, a fixed horizon and a common success probability must still be justified.')
q('Reject impossible binomial moments',r'Can a binomial variable have mean $3$ and variance $2$? Can it instead have mean $3$ and variance $5/2$?',r'The first pair gives $p=1-2/3=1/3$ and $n=3/(1/3)=9$, so it is feasible. The second gives $p=1-5/6=1/6$ and $n=18$, which is also feasible. Now compare mean three and variance $7/3$: they give $p=2/9$ and $n=27/2$, which is not an integer, so that pair is impossible in the ordinary binomial family. Checking only $0\le v\le\mu$ is necessary but insufficient. The implied trial count must be a nonnegative integer.')
q('A parameter from neighboring masses',r'For $X\sim Bin(8,p)$, suppose $P(X=3)=2P(X=2)>0$. Find $p$ and the modes.',r'The ratio is $\frac{P(X=3)}{P(X=2)}=\frac{8-2}{3}\frac p{1-p}=2\frac p{1-p}$. Thus $p/(1-p)=1$, giving $p=1/2$. Since $(n+1)p=9/2$ is not an integer, the unique mode is its floor, four. The positivity assumption rules out endpoint cases where division by a zero mass would be invalid. The ratio uses adjacent counts and the fixed value of $n$; comparing masses separated by several indices requires a product of ratios.', 'binomial-eight')
q('Tied binomial modes',r'Find all modes of $X\sim Bin(9,2/5)$ and justify the tie rather than rounding its mean.',r'The ratio from $k$ to $k+1$ equals $(9-k)2/[3(k+1)]$. It exceeds one for $k<3$, equals one at $k=3$, and is below one afterward. Therefore counts three and four are both modes. Equivalently, $(n+1)p=4$ is an integer, producing modes three and four. The mean is $18/5$, whose nearest integer happens to be four but would lose the equally probable count three. The mode is a maximizer of probability mass, not an estimate obtained by rounding expectation.')
q('A fair-count strict threshold',r'Eight fair independent coins are tossed. Find the probability of fewer than four heads without adding four separate binomial terms.',r'Reflection exchanges heads and tails, so $P(X<4)=P(X>4)$. The central mass is $P(X=4)=\binom84/2^8=70/256$. The remaining probability splits equally, giving $P(X<4)=(1-70/256)/2=93/256$. Fewer than four excludes the central count; at most four would add $70/256$. The symmetry argument requires a fair coin. It would not justify equal tails for a biased success probability, even if the threshold were located near the mean.')
q('Parity from a PGF',r'For $X\sim Bin(5,1/3)$, find $P(X\text{ is even})$ and explain the role of evaluation at minus one.',r'Write $e=P(X\text{ even})$ and $o=P(X\text{ odd})$. Then $e+o=G(1)=1$ and $e-o=G(-1)=(2/3-1/3)^5=1/243$. Solving gives $e=(1+1/243)/2=122/243$. The alternating signs select parity because $(-1)^k$ is positive at even indices and negative at odd indices. This method sums all admissible even masses at once. It is valid for a probability generating function at minus one; it is not a claim that a negative number is a probability.')
q('Condition on a fixed total',r'Six independent Bernoulli trials have common $0<p<1$. Given exactly two successes in all six, find the probability of exactly one success among the first three.',r'Every two-success set has the same unconditional weight $p^2(1-p)^4$, so conditioning makes the $\binom62=15$ sets uniform. To obtain one success in each half, choose one position from each three-position block, giving nine sets. The probability is $9/15=3/5$. The conditional first-half count is hypergeometric, not an independent binomial with probability $1/3$. The value of $p$ cancels because all conditioned configurations share it; unequal trial probabilities would destroy the uniform-set conclusion.', 'hypergeometric-small')
q('Thinning a fixed count',r'Each of six objects independently qualifies with probability $2/3$. Each qualified object independently passes with probability $1/2$. Find the final count law, mean and variance.',r'Combine qualification and passing at the object level. A final success requires both events and has probability $(2/3)(1/2)=1/3$. Independence across objects gives $Y\sim Bin(6,1/3)$, with mean two and variance $6(1/3)(2/3)=4/3$. Alternatively, condition on the qualification count $K$ and use $E[z^Y\mid K]=((1+z)/2)^K$ in its binomial PGF. Replacing $K$ by its mean four and using $Bin(4,1/2)$ preserves the mean but gives the wrong variance and support.', 'binomial-thinning')
q('Classify successes into competing labels',r'In five independent trials, an A outcome has probability $1/4$, a B outcome probability $1/2$, and otherwise neither occurs. Find the covariance of their counts and $P(A=1,B=2)$.',r'Within a trial, the A and B indicators cannot both equal one, so their covariance is $0-(1/4)(1/2)=-1/8$. Across different trials they are independent; summing gives $Cov(A,B)=-5/8$. For the joint event, choose one A position and two B positions; the remaining two are neither. Thus the probability is $\frac{5!}{1!2!2!}(1/4)(1/2)^2(1/4)^2=15/128$. Binomial marginals do not imply independent counts. Multiplying their marginal probabilities would count impossible overlapping labels.', 'classification')
q('Third factorial moment and ordinary third moment',r'For $X\sim Bin(7,2/5)$, derive $E[X^3]$ without expanding seven indicators cubically.',r'The PGF derivative gives $E[X(X-1)(X-2)]=7\cdot6\cdot5(2/5)^3=336/25$. Likewise $E[X(X-1)]=7\cdot6(2/5)^2=168/25$ and $E[X]=14/5$. The identity $X^3=X(X-1)(X-2)+3X(X-1)+X$ therefore gives $E[X^3]=336/25+504/25+70/25=182/5$. The falling-factorial moment differs from the ordinary moment. Omitting the lower-order corrections would lose repeated-index terms in a direct indicator expansion.')
q('A zero-truncated binomial law',r'For $X\sim Bin(4,1/2)$, compute $E[X\mid X>0]$ and $Var(X\mid X>0)$.',r'The conditioning probability is $15/16$. Since the excluded value is zero, the first and second raw numerator moments remain $E[X]=2$ and $E[X^2]=Var(X)+E[X]^2=5$. Thus the conditional moments are $32/15$ and $16/3$. Subtract their squared mean to obtain $16/3-1024/225=176/225$. Dividing the unconditional variance by $15/16$ is wrong because conditional variance must use the conditional center. The positive-conditioned law has support one through four and is not an ordinary binomial law.')
q('Unequal independent trial probabilities',r'Three independent trials have success probabilities $1/2,1/3,1/4$. Find the full success-count law and compare its variance with a same-mean binomial law.',r'Multiply $(1+z)/2$, $(2+z)/3$ and $(3+z)/4$. Their numerator is $6+11z+6z^2+z^3$, so the masses at zero through three are $1/4,11/24,1/4,1/24$. The mean is $13/12$ and variance $1/4+2/9+3/16=95/144$. The same-mean binomial would have $p=13/36$ and variance $3p(1-p)=299/432$, exceeding $285/432$ by $7/216$. Equal mean does not identify equal laws. The difference measures probability heterogeneity, not dependence.', 'heterogeneous')
q('Pairwise independence is insufficient',r'Let $U,V$ be independent fair bits, and let $W=U\mathbin{\mathrm{xor}}V$. Is $U+V+W$ binomial? Find its law.',r'The four equally likely pairs $(U,V)$ produce triples $000,011,101,110$. Every two coordinates are independent fair bits, but all three are not mutually independent: their parity is always even. The sum is zero with probability $1/4$ and two with probability $3/4$. Its mean is $3/2$ and variance $3/4$, the same as $Bin(3,1/2)$, yet its support excludes one and three. This counterexample simultaneously disproves inferring a binomial law from pairwise independence or from matching its first two moments.')
q('A common hidden success probability',r'Before six trials, choose $P=1/4$ or $3/4$ with equal probabilities. Conditional on P, the trials are independent. Find the count mean, variance and all-zero probability.',r'The average success probability is $1/2$ and $Var(P)=1/16$. Total variance gives $Var(S)=6(1/2)(1/2)+6\cdot5/16=27/8$, while the mean is three. The all-zero mass is $\frac12[(3/4)^6+(1/4)^6]=365/4096$. A fair binomial would have variance $3/2$ and all-zero mass $1/64$. Conditional independence does not imply unconditional independence when trials share P. The excess variance quantifies the shared-parameter contribution; it is not numerical error.', 'latent-count')
q('Reflect a biased binomial event',r'For $X\sim Bin(10,1/5)$, express $P(X\ge8)$ as a lower-tail event for another binomial variable. Why is reflecting around the mean invalid?',r'Count failures instead of successes: $Y=10-X\sim Bin(10,4/5)$. The event $X\ge8$ is exactly $Y\le2$, so its probability is $\sum_{j=0}^2\binom{10}j(4/5)^j(1/5)^{10-j}$. Reflection changes the success parameter from $p$ to $1-p$ and the threshold from eight to two. It does not assert symmetry of the original biased distribution. Its mean is two, but masses equally spaced around that mean need not agree.')
q('Distribution of the first successful position given a count',r'Ten common-probability independent trials contain exactly three successes. Given this total, find the probability that the first success is at position four.',r'Conditioning produces a uniform three-position subset of ten. Having the first success at four forces positions one through three to fail and position four to succeed. Choose the remaining two successes among positions five through ten, giving $\binom62$ configurations out of $\binom{10}3$. The probability is $15/120=1/8$. The unconditioned first-success law would be geometric, but the given total changes the law and bounds its support. This is an order statistic of a uniform subset, connecting conditional binomial positions to finite-population waiting.', 'finite-wait')
q('Positive waiting versus failure count',r'A first-success waiting variable T has mean six. Identify p, $E[T^2]$, $Var(T)$ and the mean of the number F of preceding failures.',r'For positive geometric waiting, $E[T]=1/p$, so $p=1/6$ and $q=5/6$. Its variance is $q/p^2=30$. Hence $E[T^2]=30+36=66$. The failure count is $F=T-1$, giving mean five and the same variance thirty. Subtracting one changes the mean and support but not variance. If the symbol T instead denoted failures, mean six would imply $p=1/7$; identifying the convention before solving is essential.', 'geometric-six', 'Reconstructed Oxford Prelims geometric moment exercise type; new independently worded teaching solution')
q('A shifted conditional wait',r'For positive $T\sim Geom(1/3)$, find $P(T>10\mid T>7)$ and $E[T\mid T>7]$.',r'The survival probability is $P(T>m)=(2/3)^m$. The ratio is $(2/3)^{10}/(2/3)^7=(2/3)^3=8/27$. Conditional on the first seven failures, the remaining wait has the original positive geometric law. Thus $E[T\mid T>7]=7+3=10$. This expectation includes seven elapsed trials; it is not the residual expectation three. Writing $T\ge7$ instead would condition on only six guaranteed failures and change both thresholds.')
q('A strict threshold with a noninteger bound',r'For positive $T\sim Geom(1/4)$, find $P(2.7<T\le6.2)$ and give a general integer-threshold procedure.',r'The event is $T\in\{3,4,5,6\}$, equivalently $T>2$ minus $T>6$. Its probability is $(3/4)^2-(3/4)^6=1575/4096$. For an integer-valued variable, first convert each strict or non-strict real threshold into admissible integer endpoints. Here the lower condition uses floor two, and the upper condition uses floor six. A formula involving $q^{2.7}$ would describe no event in this discrete experiment. Tail expressions should be applied only after the lattice conversion.')
q('Cap a wait without dropping failures',r'For $T\sim Geom(1/2)$ on positive integers, define $C=\min(T,4)$. Find its law and mean.',r'The masses at one, two and three remain $1/2,1/4,1/8$. At four, combine an actual fourth-trial success with every later success: $P(C=4)=P(T\ge4)=1/8$. The mean is $1/2+2/4+3/8+4/8=15/8$. The tail-sum identity gives the independent check $E[C]=\sum_{j=0}^3(1/2)^j=15/8$. Assigning only $P(T=4)=1/16$ to the endpoint loses the probability of unresolved attempts. Capping records all attempts and differs from success-only truncation.', 'censored')
q('Truncate the same wait',r'An experiment records T only when $T\le4$, where $T\sim Geom(1/2)$. Find the recorded mean and compare it with capping at four.',r'The success-by-four probability is $15/16$. The raw first-moment numerator over positions one through four is $1/2+2/4+3/8+4/16=13/8$. Dividing gives $E[T\mid T\le4]=26/15$. Its endpoint mass is $(1/16)/(15/16)=1/15$, not the capped endpoint $1/8$. Truncation discards unresolved attempts and renormalizes the retained law, while capping retains them at four. The means differ because these are different recorded populations, even though both displayed supports run from one to four.', 'truncated')
q('A failure code at zero',r'Let R equal T when $T\le4$ and zero otherwise, for positive $T\sim Geom(1/2)$. Find its mean and explain why R is not a failure-count geometric variable.',r'The zero mass is $P(T>4)=1/16$. The positive masses are $1/2,1/4,1/8,1/16$ at one through four. Their total with the zero mass is one, and the mean is $13/8$. Here zero encodes an unresolved attempt; it does not mean zero failures before a success. A failure-count geometric law would have probability $1/2$ at zero and unbounded positive support. Numeric labels alone do not determine the random variable: their observational meaning must be part of the experiment contract.', 'failure-code')
q('A geometric parity event',r'For positive $T\sim Geom(p)$, derive the probabilities of odd and even waiting times. Evaluate at $p=1/3$.',r'Odd positions have masses $p,pq^2,pq^4,\ldots$, so their sum is $p/(1-q^2)=1/(1+q)$. Even positions add one failure factor, giving $q/(1+q)$. At $p=1/3$, the probabilities are $3/5$ and $2/5$. A parity probability is generally not one half, even though both parity classes contain infinitely many integers. The earliest positions receive larger mass. The series calculation requires $p>0$; at $p=0$ there is no finite successful waiting time.')
q('Two clocks and a tie',r'Independent positive geometric waits have success probabilities $1/3$ and $1/2$. Find the probabilities that the first is smaller, they tie, and the second is smaller.',r'At position k, a strict first-clock win has probability $p_1q_1^{k-1}q_2^k$. Summing gives $p_1q_2/(1-q_1q_2)$. Here the denominator is $2/3$, so the first-clock win probability is $1/4$. A tie has probability $p_1p_2/(1-q_1q_2)=1/4$. The second-clock win is $p_2q_1/(1-q_1q_2)=1/2$. These disjoint events sum to one. Treating a discrete race as a continuous one would omit a nonzero tie probability.', 'race')
q('Minimum and maximum waits',r'For the two independent waits in the preceding race, compute the expectations of their minimum and maximum.',r'The minimum survives m trials only when both clocks do, so its survival is $(q_1q_2)^m=(1/3)^m$. It is positive geometric with success probability $2/3$, giving mean $3/2$. Pointwise, the minimum plus the maximum equals the sum of the two waits. Their individual means are three and two, hence the maximum has mean $5-3/2=7/2$. The maximum is not geometric: its survival is $q_1^m+q_2^m-(q_1q_2)^m$. Independence is needed for the product survival, not for the pointwise sum identity.', 'race')
q('Waiting with changing hazards',r'On trial i, an independent attempt succeeds with probability $p_i=1/(i+1)$. Find $P(T>m)$ and decide whether the first-success wait has finite mean.',r'Independence gives $P(T>m)=\prod_{i=1}^m(1-p_i)=\prod_{i=1}^m i/(i+1)=1/(m+1)$. This survival tends to zero, so success eventually occurs almost surely. However, the positive-integer tail-sum mean is $\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}1/(m+1)$, which diverges. Almost-sure finite completion therefore does not imply finite expected duration. The hazards change with elapsed trials, so neither a constant-p geometric law nor its memoryless formula applies.')
q('A mixture destroys memorylessness',r'Choose p equally from $1/4,3/4$ once, and then wait for a success in conditionally independent trials. Compare $P(T>2\mid T>1)$ with $P(T>1)$.',r'The initial one-trial survival is the average failure probability, $1/2$. Two-trial survival averages squared failure probabilities: $\frac12[(3/4)^2+(1/4)^2]=5/16$. Therefore conditional residual one-trial survival is $(5/16)/(1/2)=5/8$, not $1/2$. Observing a failure increases posterior weight on the low-success regime. Conditional geometric behavior given p does not establish an unconditional geometric law. The same hidden parameter persists through the experiment; choosing a fresh independent p each trial would have a different result.')
q('Recover a geometric law from memorylessness',r'A positive-integer variable T satisfies $P(T>m+n)=P(T>m)P(T>n)$ for all nonnegative integers. If $P(T>1)=3/5$, derive its full law.',r'Let $a_m=P(T>m)$; positive support gives $a_0=1$. The identity with n equal to one yields $a_{m+1}=a_m(3/5)$, so induction gives $a_m=(3/5)^m$. Taking successive survival differences produces $P(T=k)=(3/5)^{k-1}(2/5)$ for positive k. Thus T is geometric with success probability $2/5$. Its mean is $5/2$. The support and the survival identity establish the law; knowing a single tail probability alone would not suffice.')
q('The final-success coefficient',r'Independent trials succeed with probability $1/4$. Find the probability that the second success occurs exactly on trial five.',r'Trial five must succeed, and the first four trials must contain exactly one success. There are $\binom41=4$ valid arrangements, each with probability $p^2q^3$. Thus the required probability is $4(1/4)^2(3/4)^3=27/256$. Using $\binom52$ includes arrangements whose second success occurred before trial five and whose final trial failed. A fixed-horizon count of two successes and a stopped wait through two successes describe different events, although they are related by count–wait duality.', 'negative-two')
q('Count–wait duality for a cumulative event',r'For the same p, let T be the trial of the second success. Find $P(T\le5)$ without adding four waiting-time masses.',r'The second success has occurred by five exactly when a fixed five-trial binomial count is at least two. Therefore $P(T\le5)=1-(3/4)^5-5(1/4)(3/4)^4=47/128$. The two excluded events are zero and exactly one success. The equality concerns events in a common trial sequence; T itself is not binomial and the five-trial count is not negative binomial. At a threshold below the required success count, the cumulative probability is zero by support.', 'negative-two')
q('Failure-based negative-binomial moments',r'A failure count F before r successes has mean four and variance twelve under a common success probability p. Recover r and p.',r'For the failure-based convention, $\mu=rq/p$ and $v=rq/p^2$. If the mean is positive, dividing gives $v/\mu=1/p$, hence $p=1/3$. Now $q/p=2$, so $r=2$. The corresponding total-trial wait is $T=F+r$, with mean six and variance twelve. Using the total-trial formula directly on the failure mean would give an incorrect parameter. The recovered r must be a positive integer for a literal stopped-trial experiment.')
q('An independent sum with a common parameter',r'Independent waits T1 and T2 stop at the second and third successes, respectively, in separate Bernoulli experiments with the same p. Identify their sum law.',r'Each wait has PGF $(pz/(1-qz))^r$ under the total-trial convention. Independence multiplies the two PGFs, giving $(pz/(1-qz))^5$. This is the total-trial negative-binomial wait through five successes. Its mean is $5/p$ and variance $5q/p^2$. The separate experiment interpretation avoids double-counting a single timeline. If T2 instead meant the third success on the same timeline as T1, the waits would be dependent and their sum would not have this product PGF.', 'negative-convolution', 'Reconstructed Cambridge negative-binomial convolution exercise type; separate experiments and new parameters')
q('A sum with unequal success probabilities',r'Independent positive geometric waits use $p_1=1/2$ and $p_2=1/3$. Can their sum be the ordinary two-success negative-binomial wait for some p?',r'Its PGF is $p_1p_2z^2/[(1-q_1z)(1-q_2z)]$, with two distinct denominator factors. A common-p two-success negative-binomial PGF instead has a repeated denominator factor. A numerical check reaches the same conclusion: the mean is five, requiring p equal to $2/5$, which would give variance $15/2$. The actual variance is $2+6=8$, so the laws cannot agree. Independent sums retain a negative-binomial shape only when the common-probability condition is satisfied.')
q('Tied negative-binomial modes',r'For the failure count before three successes with $p=1/3$, find all modes.',r'The ratio of masses at f plus one and f is $q(f+r)/(f+1)=2(f+3)/[3(f+1)]$. It equals one when f equals three, exceeds one at smaller indices and is below one at larger indices. Therefore failure counts three and four are tied modes. Total-trial modes are obtained by adding r, giving six and seven. The failure-count mode formula involves $(r-1)q/p$; its integral value creates a tie one count earlier. Applying a binomial mode rule to this unbounded stopped count is invalid.')
q('A conditional residual wait after a partial count',r'A trial sequence has common success probability $1/3$. Given that the first five trials contain exactly one success, how much longer on average is needed to reach three successes in total?',r'The conditioning depends only on the first five trials. Independence leaves the future sequence unchanged, and two additional successes are required. Its extra wait is a total-trial negative-binomial variable with r equal to two and mean $2/(1/3)=6$. Thus the conditional total finishing-trial expectation is eleven. It is not nine, the unconditional mean wait for three successes. Elapsed trials and remaining successes are separate state variables. If past information also updated a shared unknown success parameter, the future law would need posterior mixing.')
q('The second moment of a stopped wait',r'For the total wait T through four successes with $p=2/3$, find $E[T^2]$ and the covariance of T with its failure count F.',r'The mean is $r/p=6$, and variance is $rq/p^2=4(1/3)/(4/9)=3$. Consequently $E[T^2]=3+36=39$. Since $F=T-4$, covariance is $Cov(T,F)=Cov(T,T-4)=Var(T)=3$. The same random variable shifted by a constant is perfectly positively correlated when variance is nonzero. T and F are not independent merely because one counts all trials and the other only failures. Their deterministic relation should be used before attempting a joint probability calculation.')
q('A binomial horizon mistaken for a stopping horizon',r'An observer reports exactly three successes in ten trials with probability p. Another observer reports the third success occurring at trial ten. Compare their event probabilities.',r'The fixed-horizon event has probability $\binom{10}3p^3q^7$. The stopped event forces the tenth trial to be a success and chooses the other two among the first nine, giving $\binom92p^3q^7$. Their ratio is $\binom92/\binom{10}3=3/10$, independent of p when both probabilities are positive. Given exactly three successes among ten, each of the three success positions is uniformly distributed over the ten positions, so the final trial is successful with probability $3/10$. The distinction is a support and combinatorial constraint, not a change in trial probability.')
q('Success before a failure budget is exhausted',r'With success probability $2/3$, stop when either three successes or two failures have occurred. Find the probability that the successes win.',r'Successes win exactly when the third success occurs with zero or one preceding failures. The two probabilities are $p^3$ and $\binom31p^3q$, totaling $p^3(1+3q)=16/27$. Equivalently, among the first four trials at least three are successes, whose binomial probability is $4p^3q+p^4=16/27$. The event equivalence works because either stopping condition must occur within four trials. Continuing after a losing second failure and counting later successes would change the experiment.')
q('An exponential tilt of a geometric law',r'Let F have failure-based geometric parameter p. Reweight its masses by $a^f$ with $a\ge0$. When does normalization exist, and what is the resulting law?',r'The unnormalized weights are $p(aq)^f$. Their sum is $p/(1-aq)$ exactly when $aq<1$. Dividing gives masses $(1-aq)(aq)^f$, a failure-based geometric law with new success parameter $1-aq$. At $a=0$, all mass is at zero; when $aq\ge1$ the sum diverges and no normalized probability law exists. The tilt changes the distribution of the entire count. This algebra explains geometric posterior updates under an exponentially decreasing likelihood, without treating a likelihood itself as a normalized prior.', 'family-posterior')
q('A full hypergeometric probability',r'Three items are sampled uniformly without replacement from ten, of which four are marked. Find the probability of exactly two marks, the mean and variance.',r'There are $\binom{10}3=120$ equally likely subsets. Favorable subsets choose two of four marks and one of six unmarked items, giving $6\cdot6=36$, so the probability is $3/10$. The mean is $3(4/10)=6/5$. Variance includes the finite-population factor: $3(4/10)(6/10)(7/9)=14/25$. Replacement would instead give binomial variance $18/25$ and a different mass at two. Uniform subsets and replacement assumptions must be established before applying either model.', 'hypergeometric-small')
q('Find the effective support first',r'Five objects are drawn without replacement from eight, six of which are marked. Find the full count law.',r'At most two unmarked objects exist, so at least three of the five draws must be marked; at most five can be marked. The support is therefore three, four and five. The masses are $\binom63\binom22/\binom85=5/14$, $\binom64\binom21/\binom85=15/28$, and $\binom65\binom20/\binom85=3/28$. They sum to one. A nominal range zero through five is harmless only if impossible counts have explicitly zero mass; listing them as feasible outcomes obscures the finite-pool constraint.', 'hypergeometric-dense')
q('Draw the whole population',r'A hypergeometric experiment samples all N objects from a population with K marks. What becomes of its mean, variance and mass formula, including $N=1$?',r'The count is deterministically K. Its mean is K and variance zero. For $N>1$, the usual variance factor $(N-n)/(N-1)$ is zero at $n=N$. At $N=1$, that ratio is formally zero divided by zero and must not be evaluated; the direct constant-law description remains valid. The subset probability is one at K because the full population has only one possible sample. Formula endpoints should be handled by their experiments, not by cancelling or assigning arbitrary values to undefined expressions.')
q('Count marked items left behind',r'If X counts marks in a uniform sample of n from N with K marks, identify the distribution of $K-X$ and derive the relation between the two variances.',r'The complement of a uniform n-subset is a uniform $(N-n)$-subset. Therefore $K-X$ is hypergeometric with sample size $N-n$, population N and mark count K. Its mean is $K-nK/N=(N-n)K/N$ and its variance equals that of X because subtraction from a constant leaves variance unchanged. This also follows by inserting $N-n$ into the finite-population variance formula. It does not imply the sample and its complement have independent counts; their counts sum deterministically to K.')
assert len(Q)==43
(B/'s_distributions-questions.json').write_text(json.dumps(Q,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n',encoding='utf-8')
print('Authored first 43 independently explained distribution problems.')
